### Database design for Haarisah

| FR | Table | Columns (type) | Keys and relationships | Constraints |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| FR3, FR4 | `collection_entries` | `id (INTEGER)`<br>`user_id (INTEGER)`<br>`game_id (INTEGER)`<br>`status (VARCHAR(6))`<br>`added_at (DATETIME)`<br>`updated_at (DATETIME)` | PK(Primary key): `id`<br>FK(Foreign Key): `user_id → users.id` with `ON DELETE RESTRICT`<br>FK: `game_id → games.id` with `ON DELETE RESTRICT`<br>One user and one game per entry. A user or game can have many entries. | `user_id` and `game_id` are `NOT NULL`.<br>`status NOT NULL` with `CHECK (status IN ('owned', 'played', 'wanted'))`.<br>`UNIQUE (user_id, game_id)` keeps one entry per player per game.<br>`added_at` and `updated_at` are `NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`. The API keeps `added_at` fixed and updates `updated_at` when the status changes. |

**Design decision:** Collection Timestamps. I chose to keep added_at fixed when a game’s status changes and update updated_at instead. The alternative was to reset added_at on each status change. That would lose the date the player first added the game. Keeping both timestamps preserves the original addition date and the date of the latest change.